A hand-edited .yoyo/memory.json that no longer parses now stays as it is. Before this, one trailing comma plus /remember wiped every saved memory without a warning; now the write is refused and the file's bytes don't change. It's committed as af6eb4a8, titled "Part of #1216".

What I saw on the built binary (driving the REPL through a pseudo-terminal in a temp repo, using the same file):
• Before: ✓ Remembered: "new note" (1 total memories), and "keep me" was erased.
• After: `not saved, nothing was written: .yoyo/memory.json is not valid JSON: trailing comma at line 1 column 67. Fix the JSON or move the file aside, then retry.` The file was unchanged.

What changed:
• Anything that saves memories now refuses a broken file. That's /remember, /forget, and the automatic notes from watch mode, which includes dropping the oldest ones at the cap of 20. The automatic path skips the save and warns once per process.
• A file that exists but can't be read is also refused. Only a missing file still counts as an empty store.
• Readers that only display memories carry on, but no longer silently. That's the startup prompt, /memories and /retry. They show nothing and print warning: project memories not loaded - … instead.

Tests:
• Seven new tests in src/memory_parse_guard_tests.rs cover: the broken file with /remember, /forget, and automatic notes including the drop at the cap; the same file without the comma (both entries kept); a missing file (created); an unreadable file; and the error naming the file and line.
• One existing test moved there from memory.rs unchanged, so memory.rs stays under its 2000-line cap (1993 lines).
• Positive control: I switched the strict parse back to "treat as empty", and the four tests that use the broken file failed by name. The three that don't stayed green. After restoring it, everything passed.
• fmt, clippy with -D warnings, and the full cargo test all pass.

Other stores: I checked other stores yoyo loads and writes back for the same bug and found no other live case. The session todo list and revisit list already refuse a bad file. The trust store and /config set edit the existing text rather than replacing it. Risk weights are rebuilt from their source data, and the risk logs are append-only. This check was based on grep, so a store whose loader and writer don't match those search patterns could have been missed.

Not done, and why it's only "Part of":
• The "not loaded" warning prints on every load, not once per session. In the pty run it appeared twice, the second time after /remember, and I didn't trace which reload causes that.
• The census was grep-based, as above.

Both are on #1216 for the owner to decide whether to close it.

The before/after runs, the control result and the census table are posted on #1216. The docs (docs/src/usage/commands.md) now say what happens to a broken memory.json. Two things outside the planned files: I deleted save_memories(), which no longer had any caller and failed clippy, and updated a comment in src/commands.rs that named it.
